# Audio PII Anonymizer — Technical Write-Up

## 1. Approach & What We Built

Customer-support calls in Indian languages present challenges that break standard English audio redaction pipelines: **code-switching** (English digits and PIN codes inside Hindi or Tamil sentences), **grouped and repeated digits** (`"double ஆறு"`, `"triple five"`), **colloquial spoken numerals** (spoken Tamil `"ஒன்னு, ரெண்டு, மூணு, அஞ்சு"` alongside literary forms), **uncased Indic scripts**, and **agent read-backs** where a single first name (`"धन्यवाद प्रिया जी"`, `"நன்றி கார்த்திக்"`) or partial number is repeated later in the call.

To meet the three core requirements—**no PII leaks**, **minimal collateral damage to surrounding speech**, and **byte-for-byte repeatability**—we built a deterministic, five-stage pipeline (`pii_anonymizer`) whose primary output is an independent copy of the recording (`<stem>_redacted.wav`) with every PII interval overwritten by a speaker-loudness-matched `1000 Hz` beep:

1. **Native-Dtype Audio I/O (`audio.py`):** Decodes `.wav` and `.mp3` inputs while preserving native integer/float PCM representations for WAV files, cloning the sample buffer (`Audio.copy()`) so every sample outside a beep span remains `100%` bit-identical to the input and the frame count, sample rate, and channel count match with `0.0 ms` drift.
2. **Deterministic Speech Recognition & Content Cache (`asr.py`, `cache.py`):** Runs `faster-whisper` (`CTranslate2`, `int8` on CPU / `float16` on CUDA) with greedy/beam decoding at `temperature=[0.0]`, `condition_on_previous_text=False`, and fixed Silero VAD thresholds. Word-level transcripts are cached under `SHA-256(audio_bytes + model + language + decode_params)` so repeat runs execute in `0.05 s` without loading Whisper.
3. **Sub-Word Tokenization & Cross-Script Normalization (`tokens.py`, `textnorm.py`):** Splits hyphenated/grouped numerals (`"9845-012-345"`) with character-proportional timestamps, normalizes Unicode nukta/chandrabindu/zero-width joiners, and computes a script-agnostic `phonetic_key` mapping Devanagari, Tamil, Kannada, and Latin spellings to a shared consonant-vowel skeleton (`"Karthik"` == `"கார்த்திக்"` == `"कार्तिक"` == `"kartik"`).
4. **Declarative Language-Pack Detectors (`numbers.py`, `names.py`, `languages/*.yaml`):**
   - *Number FSM:* Parses mixed native/English digits, `10–99` compounds (`"चौरानवे"` = `94`), combining tens + units, hundreds, `"double"`/`"triple"` modifiers, filler words inside pauses, and agent partial read-backs near `mobile`/`pincode` keywords.
   - *Five-Signal Name Engine:* Combines (a) strong introduction cues (`"मेरा नाम"`, `"என் பெயர்"`, `"my name is"`), (b) conversational frames (`"मैं <X> बोल रहा हूँ"`), (c) pre/post honorifics (`"<X> जी"`, `"<X> சார்"`, `"श्री <X>"`), (d) cross-script phonetic gazetteer lookup, and (e) **call-wide entity memory** that redacts every subsequent agent or customer repeat of any discovered name token.
5. **10 ms RMS Acoustic Boundary Alignment & Speaker-Matched Beep Synthesis (`spans.py`, `audio.py`):** Refines noisy ASR word timestamps against the raw `10 ms` RMS energy contour of the waveform, pads boundaries safely (`80 ms` pre / `100 ms` post), and replaces (never mixes) PII samples with a `1000 Hz` sine wave shaped with `5 ms` raised-cosine ramps and matched to the active speech RMS of the speaker in that span.

