## 2. What We Tried & What Failed

To evaluate redaction objectively rather than solely by ear, we built [`scripts/evaluate.py`](scripts/evaluate.py), which scores redacted audio against exact segment-level ground truth on two competing metrics:
- **`LEAK` (ms):** voiced speech energy inside a ground-truth PII span not covered by a beep (target: `0 ms`; a single leaked digit is `~300–500 ms`).
- **`EXTRA` (s):** audio outside ground-truth PII spans covered by a beep beyond a `0.25 s` transition margin (target: as close to `0.0 s` as possible).

Iterative testing exposed six concrete failures that shaped the final system:

### 2.1 Off-the-Shelf Text NER Failed on Uncased Indic ASR & Single-Name Agent Repeats
- **What we tried:** Running standard multilingual NER over Whisper transcripts.
- **What failed:** Indic scripts lack capitalization and ASR output lacks reliable punctuation. Single-token agent acknowledgments (`"धन्यवाद प्रिया जी"`, `"நன்றி கார்த்திக்"`) were missed (leaking the customer's first name), while lowering confidence thresholds caused false-positive beeps on polite verbs and common nouns.
- **Resolution:** Built the five-signal name detector in `names.py` with **call-wide entity memory**. Once `"प्रिया शर्मा"` is introduced after `"मेरा नाम"`, both `"प्रिया"` and `"शर्मा"` are registered by normalized form and `phonetic_key`, guaranteeing that `"धन्यवाद प्रिया जी"` is redacted even without an introduction cue.

### 2.2 SOV Sentence-Final Verbs Triggered Weak Name Cues
- **What we tried:** Allowing `weak_cues` (`"नाम"`, `"பெயர்"`) to accept a following token whenever it appeared at the end of a sentence (`sentence_end=True`).
- **What failed:** Hindi and Tamil are Subject-Object-Verb (SOV) languages. In the agent prompt `"अपना नाम बताइए।"` (transcribed by `large-v3-turbo` as `"अपना नाम बताए।"`), the verb `"बताए।"` immediately follows `"नाम"` at a sentence boundary. Treating `sentence_end` as a valid terminator misclassified `"बताए"` as a customer name and then beeped `"बताए"` again via call memory in `"मोबाइल नंबर बताए।"`.
- **Resolution:** Restricted `weak_cues` so `sentence_end` alone never validates an unknown word; a weak cue now requires either an explicit lexical terminator (`"है"`, `"जी"`, `"தான்"`) or a phonetic gazetteer match (whereas strong cues like `"मेरा नाम"` still accept arbitrary out-of-vocabulary names), alongside expanded verb-inflection stopwords.

### 2.3 Whisper DTW Timestamp Compression & Turn-Silence Gluing (`190–520 ms` Digit Leaks)
- **What we tried:** Using Whisper's raw `[word.start, word.end]` timestamps with `±100 ms` padding, clamped strictly to `[prev_word.end, next_word.start]` so neighboring words would not be clipped.
- **What failed:** Two systematic Whisper DTW alignment bugs caused `190–520 ms` leaks on final digits:
  1. Whisper converts multi-second spoken digit sequences (`"पाँच छह शून्य शून्य तीन चार"`, `2.4 s` of speech) into a single written numeral token `"560034।"` whose reported DTW window often covers only `0.5–1.2 s`.
  2. Whisper routinely sets `next_word.start == digit_word.end`, absorbing the final `200–500 ms` of the last spoken digit *plus* the following `600 ms` turn pause into the next turn's first word (`"ठीक"`, `"சரி"`, `"धन्यवाद"`). Clamping at `next_word.start` left the final digit (`"चार"`, `"ஏழு"`, `"छह"`) audible.
- **Resolution (`spans.py:_EnergyIndex`):** Built a four-step `10 ms` RMS acoustic aligner: (1) `trim_outer_silence` strips dead turn-silence glued onto PII tokens; (2) `true_offset` / `true_onset` scans adjacent non-PII tokens for internal silence valleys `>= 280 ms` (impossible inside one spoken word, always an absorbed turn boundary) to locate the neighbor word's true acoustic edge; (3) `extend_with_gaps` bridges grouped-digit pauses (`<= 420 ms`) up to those true acoustic guardrails; and (4) `finish_voiced_burst` walks to the end of any mid-syllable boundary before short `< 280 ms` intra-turn pauses while protecting at least `180 ms` of the neighbor word. Leaks dropped from `520 ms` to **`0–10 ms`**.

### 2.4 Abandoned Heuristic: Gating Acoustic Extension by Span Duration (`min_full_dur`)
- **What we tried:** Skipping `extend_with_gaps` whenever a detected mobile span already exceeded `min_full_dur = 3.6 s`, hoping to shave `~0.1 s` of extra beep padding.
- **What failed:** On `sample_1_hindi.wav` under greedy `large-v3` decoding, the first nine digits spanned `3.8 s` (exceeding `3.6 s`), while the tenth digit sat across a short pause that Whisper glued onto the next turn—re-introducing a **`280 ms` leak**.
- **Resolution:** Reverted the duration gate immediately. Running `extend_with_gaps` unconditionally within `true_onset`/`true_offset` acoustic guardrails guarantees zero digit leaks across all models.

### 2.5 Code-Mixed Repeaters (`"double ஆறு"` -> `"double R"`), Lexicon Collisions & Tamil Alveolar `ன`
- **What we tried:** Naive YAML number lexicons, stripping weak homophone digits (`"to"`, `"for"`, `"नो"`, `"r"`) from run edges whenever `>= 6` digits remained, and default ITRANS transliteration for Tamil.
- **What failed:**
  1. In Tamil Sample 2, `"double ஆறு"` (`ஆறு` = `6`) was transcribed by `large-v3-turbo` as `"double R"`.
  2. Duplicate YAML keys (`"11".."19"` listed twice in `hi.yaml` for native Hindi teens and English-transliterated teens) silently overwrote the native Hindi teen words (`"ग्यारह".."उन्नीस"`).
  3. Tamil alveolar `ன` (`U+0BA9` in `"ராஜன்"`) transliterated to `^n` in ITRANS; stripping punctuation dropped `^` and caused final-nasal mismatches against `"Rajan"`.
- **Resolution:** Added `"r"`/`"ar"` as a weak digit for `6` in `ta.yaml` and forbade `_trim_weak_edges` from stripping any weak digit governed by a `REPEAT` token (`"double R"`); merged teen lists and added an automated `LexiconError` collision check on pack load (`languages/__init__.py`); folded Tamil `ன` (`\u0ba9`) to `ந` (`\u0ba8`) before ITRANS in `textnorm.py`.

### 2.6 CPU Cold-Start Latency & Two-Speaker Beep Loudness Mismatch
- **What we tried:** Running full 32-layer `Whisper large-v3` (`beam_size=5`) on every run (`~48 s`/call on CPU) and using a single file-wide `speech_rms` amplitude for all beeps.
- **What failed:** `48 s` per call felt sluggish during live iteration, and a single global beep level sounded slightly loud over a quiet customer (`-22.9 dBFS`) or slightly soft over a louder agent (`-15.1 dBFS`).
- **Resolution:** Adopted `large-v3-turbo` (`fast` preset, 4-layer decoder, `int8` beam 5) as the default—cutting cold ASR time by **2.7x** (`RTF 0.42`, `~9–11 s` per 30 s call) with `0` leaks—paired with the SHA-256 `TranscriptCache` (`0.05 s` repeat runs). In `audio.py:apply_beeps`, each beep is now matched to the **local active speech RMS** of that specific span (bounded smoothly around global speech RMS), achieving **`0.0–0.1 dB`** loudness delta on every speaker.

